Jeeves wrote:
> Hej alla,
> 
> Finally ascended a character! After having played the game for several
> years off and on, since version 3.2.3, I went an an all-out binge for the
> last 3 weeks (with interruptions for final exams), and beat the RNG at its
> own game. I'll describe the highlights of my dungeon crawl for your
> edification and/or enjoyment.

Congratulations, and welcome to the demigod[ess] bar!

> I think Archaeologists are somewhat underappreciated. = ) The ability to
> dig and the intrinsic speed (and later searching) came in IMMENSE handy.

They're my preferred class, and also my first ascension.

> Rings
> b - a cursed ring of invisibility named nazgul (on left hand)

You're better getting inrinsic invisible, or #invoking the Orb of Detection.

> Wands
> 
> D - an uncursed wand of cancellation (0:4)
> H - an uncursed wand of polymorph (0:1)
> 
> I cannot overemphasize how important these two wands were to my survival.
> I used teleportation wands to get rid of the eels on Medusa's level, true,
> but where there is less water (such as in Rodney's Tower), polymorphing the
> krakens into whatever (which usually sinks like a stone) was a better idea.

/oTeleport are also useful.

> The cancellation wands were put to good use on the rust monsters,
> disenchanters, green slimes, and other selected monsters to make them much
> less threatening. 

Genociding them is also a good option... actually, by the time I find 
R's as an arc, I usually have an attack spell or two and use them (arcs 
can be decent spellcasters, you know). /oPoly can be more useful for 
other things...

> Quest
> 
> The Minion of Huhetotl gave me a good run for my money, but he went down
> because I had potions of full healing and an amulet of life saving, and he
> didn't. 

Using a cold attack is a good option against him; the very first thing I 
usually do when I see him is throwing a !oParalysis, then attacking with 
cold (wand, spell, whatever).

> The Orb struck me as pretty useless, since by that time I had a
> cloak of MR and intrinisc telepathy and it's really heavy, but I decided
> not to leave it behind just in case. 

It is actually the most useless quest artifact... I wish the DevTeam 
give it a good #invoke effect. Note that it still gives you half damage 
when attacked by spells, tho.

> Took medusa out with the mirror (stumbling about blind and
> whacking away didn't seem like it would be much fun).

Erhm... mirror?

> Hell
> 
> Let me begin by saying that I encountered a Lich on DL 11 somehow, and
> having half my inventory cursed and my mithril disintegrated motivated me
> to genocide them and all their kin as soon as I found the first scroll.
> However, that was all I genocided. The mind flayers were no picnic, but

If you can live with them, they're useful to boost your Int.

> Demons
> 
> Only Orcus posed any real challenge. He summoned Yeenoghu, then ran away
> and skulked about. It took our Hero a very long time to find him again. 

This is why I prefer lawful arcs; Grayswandir against demons.

> The Wizard's Tower
> 
> Rodney was easily the worst thing I had to deal with in my entire game. I
> was never able to kill him by direct combat (not once!) and had to resort
> to the c corpse or the wand of death. I had a c corpse going into his
> tower, but he of course resurrected incessantly, and stole the Amulet (and
> the Orb) multiple times.

Good thing you had the [CoMR.

> Astral Plane
> 
> Despite putting on the ring of regeneration, this level was tough. I hurled

At this point, the =oReg is not all that useful... I'd rather use =oFA 
and/or =oIncrDmg.

